course: dictionary
lesson: 7933
title: Realistically: thinking true to life
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Realistically, we won't finish the whole book today.» Realistically starts a sensible thought. It asks you to think about what can really happen, not what you wish would happen. You might wish you could finish, but realistically you will not.
explain: Realistically is an adverb, so it tells us how we think or how something is done. It has two meanings: thinking sensibly about what is likely, or making something look real and true to life. Ask yourself: is this about wishing, or about what is likely? Then swap in "sensibly" or "truthfully" to check.
explain: The Picasso sentence uses the second meaning: «Picasso was trained from a young child and could draw very realistically as a teenager.» The clue is the rest of the sentence. He could draw things so they looked true to life, and later he developed Cubism with Georges Braque, showing things from several sides at once.
fact: «Realistically» is an adverb: it tells how we think about something or how something looks.
fact: «Realistically» means thinking about what can really happen, not what you wish for.
fact: «Realistically» can also mean in a way that looks real or true to life, as in a drawing.
fact: The phrase «realistically speaking» is used to say what is probably true.
fact: «Picasso could draw very realistically as a teenager; later he developed Cubism with Georges Braque.»
exercise: «Realistically, we won't finish today.» What does realistically mean here? | Drawn in bright colours | Quickly and loudly | Wishing very hard for something | *Thinking about what can really happen
exercise: Picasso could draw very «realistically» as a teenager. What does it mean? | *In a way that looks true to life | Very fast and messy | Without using any paper | In a silly, made-up way
exercise: Mia says, «Realistically, I can't fly to the moon by lunch.» What is she doing? | *Thinking sensibly about what can really happen | Drawing the moon in detail | Wishing for something impossible | Telling a funny joke
test: Which phrase starts a sensible thought about what can really happen? | Madly, the bus will fly | Quietly, the bus was red | *Realistically, the bus will be late | Happily, the bus was blue
test: In the Picasso sentence, what does «realistically» describe? | Music that is loud and fast | A cartoon with silly shapes | A race that someone runs | *A drawing that looks true to life
exam: What is the best check for the meaning of realistically? | Count the letters in the word | Look only at the first letter | Skip the sentence and guess | *Swap in "sensibly" or "truthfully" and see if it still makes sense
